ZeroClaw Architecture Plan
==========================

Goal
- Build a browser-based 3D SPH fluid playground with React/TypeScript UI, Three.js rendering, a worker-backed simulation core, local scene presets, and export tooling.

Recommended project structure
- `src/main.tsx`: app bootstrap.
- `src/App.tsx`: top-level layout shell composition.
- `src/lib/utils.ts`: shared UI helpers from shadcn/ui.
- `src/types/`
  - `scene.ts`: container, obstacles, emitter, preset, visualization mode.
  - `simulation.ts`: particle state views, parameter structs, stats, worker messages.
  - `ui.ts`: control-bar and panel view models if needed.
- `src/store/`
  - `sceneStore.ts`: Zustand state for editable scene, sim controls, selected preset, visualization mode.
- `src/sim/`
  - `kernels.ts`: poly6, spiky gradient, viscosity laplacian.
  - `spatialHash.ts`: neighbor lookup grid.
  - `particles.ts`: particle arrays/buffers and initialization helpers.
  - `forces.ts`: density, pressure, force accumulation.
  - `integrator.ts`: semi-implicit Euler + boundary handling.
  - `Simulation.ts`: orchestration entry point for the solver on the worker side.
- `src/worker/`
  - `protocol.ts`: shared message and payload definitions, imported by both main thread and worker.
  - `simulationWorker.ts`: worker host bridging messages to `Simulation`.
- `src/render/`
  - `Renderer.ts`: Three.js renderer/scene/camera/resize lifecycle.
  - `controls.ts`: OrbitControls setup.
  - `container.ts`: container wireframe mesh generation/update.
  - `particles.ts`: particle points or instanced mesh rendering.
  - `obstacles.ts`: obstacle mesh generation/update.
  - `lighting.ts`: default light rig and material setup.
  - `colorModes.ts`: speed/density/pressure visualization mapping.
- `src/components/`
  - `layout/`: top bar, side panel, canvas shell.
  - `controls/`: play/pause/step/reset/speed bar.
  - `panels/`: scene editor, simulation params, preset manager.
  - `overlays/`: stats overlay, onboarding, error banners.
- `src/features/export/`
  - `capture.ts`: PNG frame capture.
  - `video.ts`: WebM recording flow and guards.
- `src/presets/`
  - `defaultScenes.ts`: shipped scenes and onboarding defaults.
  - `storage.ts`: localStorage serialization/import/export helpers.
- `src/test/`: shared test helpers.
- `e2e/`: Playwright specs for happy path and export flow.

Shared type strategy
- Keep `src/types/scene.ts` and `src/types/simulation.ts` framework-agnostic and serializable.
- Prefer plain objects and typed arrays at the simulation boundary.
- Define worker protocol once in `src/worker/protocol.ts` and reuse it from worker, client, and tests to avoid drift.
- Store authoritative editable scene state in Zustand; derive render state and worker inputs from selectors/adapters instead of duplicating shape.

Core design decisions
- Simulation runs off the main thread in a Web Worker beginning with issue #17; earlier simulation modules should already avoid DOM/React dependencies so they drop into the worker without refactor.
- Use structure-of-arrays for particle state (`Float32Array`/`Uint32Array`) instead of arrays of objects. This keeps neighbor search, density/force passes, and worker transfer efficient.
- Treat scene description separately from runtime particle buffers. Scene data is durable/serializable; particle buffers are ephemeral runtime state.
- Use a single `SimulationClient` abstraction on the main thread to hide worker lifecycle, stepping, reset, and state snapshots.
- Start rendering particles with `THREE.Points` for the smoke path; keep the render adapter isolated so later instanced spheres remain possible without changing the simulation API.
- Use conservative parameter clamping and particle-count safety rails in store selectors and worker validation, not only in UI inputs.
- Keep export features layered on top of the existing renderer/canvas rather than introducing a second render path.

Issue dependency map
- Foundation: #1 -> #2 -> #3 -> #4 -> #5.
- Rendering base: #6 -> #7 -> #8 -> #9.
- Simulation core: #10 -> #11 -> #12 -> #13 -> #14 -> #15 -> #16.
- Worker integration: #17 -> #18 -> #19.
- App state and scene model: #20.
- Scene content features: #21 -> #22 -> #23.
- UI shell and controls: #24 -> #25 -> #26 -> #27 -> #28 -> #29.
- Persistence/export: #30 -> #31 -> #32 -> #33.
- End-to-end coverage: #34 and #35 after their respective features land.
- Polish/documentation: #36 -> #37 -> #38.

Cross-cutting implementation notes
- Issue #20 should reconcile the earlier worker/client APIs with the UI control model; do not let UI components talk directly to Three.js or worker objects.
- Issue #21 should reuse obstacle geometry definitions for both rendering and collision to prevent divergence.
- Issue #30 should serialize only durable scene + parameter state, never transient worker buffers.
- Issue #34 and #35 should rely on stable `data-testid` hooks introduced with the relevant UI features rather than retrofitting selectors later.
- Issue #37 should centralize validation utilities so UI forms, preset import, and worker messages all enforce the same limits.

Suggested acceptance direction for future sessions
- Each issue should preserve compile-and-run behavior, even when a feature is only a scaffold.
- Public boundaries likely to survive the full project:
  - `SceneState` / `ScenePreset`
  - `SimulationParams`
  - `SimulationSnapshot`
  - `WorkerRequest` / `WorkerResponse`
  - `SimulationClient`
